FIELD NOTES
===========

1. WHAT WAS LITERALLY MEASURED
------------------------------

Instrument and data sources
- A heterogeneous annual-resolution multiproxy network:
  - dendroclimatic records and principal-component summaries of dense tree-ring networks;
  - ice-core and ice-melt indicators;
  - coral records;
  - long historical records;
  - long instrumental temperature and precipitation series.
- Instrumental calibration target:
  - monthly gridded land-air and sea-surface temperature data;
  - 1,082 nearly continuous grid-point series;
  - annualized for analysis;
  - 1902–1995 data availability;
  - 1902–1980 calibration/training interval.
- Statistical representation:
  - principal component analysis of the instrumental temperature field;
  - proxy indicators calibrated against retained instrumental principal components;
  - year-by-year inverse reconstruction of retained components;
  - reconstructed spatial fields and derived Northern Hemisphere mean anomalies.

Population / geography
- Widely distributed proxy sites, but spatial coverage was uneven.
- The selected claim concerns the areally weighted Northern Hemisphere mean, not each location, not a hemispherically uniform trend, and not necessarily the global mean.
- The authors expressly state that hemispheric means are not associated with globally or hemispherically uniform trends.

Time period
- Reconstruction presented back to AD 1400.
- Information and retained dimensionality decline backward:
  - 112 indicators by 1820;
  - 93 by 1760;
  - 74 by 1700;
  - 57 by 1600;
  - 24 by 1450;
  - 22 by 1400.
- Only the first eigenvector was retained for 1400–1449.
- Instrumental annual values were used for the recent period, with the paper mentioning a newly calculated 1997 value not displayed in Figure 5.

Unit of analysis
- Annual Northern Hemisphere mean temperature anomaly, relative to the 1902–1980 calibration mean.
- The selected comparison ranks individual recent years against reconstructed individual years back to 1400.

Uncertainty procedure stated by the paper
- Calibration residuals were examined for heteroscedasticity and approximate Gaussian behavior.
- The spectra were described as approximately white.
- Reconstruction uncertainties were calculated by assuming unresolved variance was Gaussian-distributed over time.
- Error bars widened backward as the proxy network became sparser and calibrated less variance.

Source locators
- Abstract and data: Nature 392, p. 779.
- Assumptions and calibration: pp. 780–781.
- Backward-declining network skill: pp. 781–782.
- Temperature reconstruction and selected claim: pp. 783–784.
- Calibration and uncertainty methods: p. 786.


2. STRONGEST CLAIM THE SOURCE ITSELF MAKES
------------------------------------------

Most precise version:
“Taking into account the uncertainties in our NH reconstruction … it appears that the years 1990, 1995 and now 1997 … each show anomalies that are greater than any other year back to 1400 at 3 standard errors, or roughly a 99.7% level of certainty.”

Location:
Results / “Temperature reconstructions,” crossing pp. 783–784.

Hedge and qualifiers preserved:
- “Taking into account the uncertainties”
- “it appears”
- Northern Hemisphere mean
- annual years
- back to 1400
- 3 standard errors / roughly 99.7%

Abstract version:
“Northern Hemisphere mean annual temperatures for three of the past eight years are warmer than any other year since (at least) AD 1400.”

Difference in prominence/precision:
The abstract gives a categorical ranking without the numerical confidence statement. The results section supplies the more specific “3 standard errors” and “roughly 99.7%” formulation. Because the prompt requires targeting the most precisely quantified version, Field 6 uses the results claim.


3. WEAKER DEFENSIBLE CLAIM THE EVIDENCE ACTUALLY SUPPORTS
----------------------------------------------------------

Author reconstruction from Field 1:

Using MBH98’s specified proxy network, calibration period, retained principal components, reconstruction procedure, and uncertainty assumptions, its estimated Northern Hemisphere annual-mean anomalies place 1990, 1995, and 1997 above all reconstructed annual values from AD 1400 onward.

Necessary scope limits:
- “estimated” and “reconstructed,” not directly measured before widespread instrumentation;
- Northern Hemisphere annual mean, not global temperature;
- annual ranking, not automatically a statement about decades or longer comparable periods;
- AD 1400 onward, not the full millennium;
- conditional on the proxy network, model specification, retained components, and uncertainty construction;
- not a statement of uniform regional warmth;
- not, on its own, a causal attribution to greenhouse gases or human activity.

This field does not decide whether the model or uncertainty assumptions succeed. It states the claim shape directly matched to the operationalization.


4. BROADER CLAIM ATTACHED DOWNSTREAM
-------------------------------------

A. ASSESSMENT / SYNTHESIS — NATIONAL RESEARCH COUNCIL (2006)

Provenance: RETRIEVED.

Restatement:
The NRC summary says the 1998 and 1999 reconstructions attracted attention because “the authors concluded that the Northern Hemisphere was warmer during the late 20th century than at any other time during the past millennium.”

Layer:
Assessment / synthesis.

Attribution check:
- Could MBH98 alone support the millennium period? No. Its useful reconstruction begins at AD 1400.
- Who is cited? The NRC describes the 1998 and 1999 reconstructions together.
- Does the document distinguish scopes elsewhere? Yes. It separately refers to the “original conclusions by Mann et al. (1999)” that the 1990s were likely the warmest decade and 1998 the warmest year “in at least a millennium.”
- Classification: SYNTHESIS ATTRIBUTION, not MISATTRIBUTED.
- Verification state: CROSS-SOURCE-CONFIRMED.

A second NRC formulation calls “[t]he basic conclusion of Mann et al. (1998, 1999)” that late-twentieth-century Northern Hemisphere warmth was unprecedented during at least 1,000 years.

Document-level result:
Again, the surrounding document distinguishes the 1999 annual/decadal millennium conclusion. The joint citation is best recorded as synthesis of a same-author research program, not as a demonstrated assignment of the 1,000-year scope to MBH98 alone.

B. ASSESSMENT — IPCC THIRD ASSESSMENT REPORT (2001)

Provenance: RETRIEVED.

Restatement:
“Taking into account these substantial uncertainties, Mann et al. (1999) concluded that the 1990s were likely to have been the warmest decade, and 1998 the warmest year, of the millennium.”

Layer:
Assessment.

Attribution check:
The millennium annual/decadal claim is attributed to MBH99, the successor paper extending the reconstruction to AD 1000. It is therefore ATTRIBUTED ELSEWHERE relative to MBH98, not inflation by MBH98.

Verification note:
The IPCC passage and bibliography were retrieved. The 1999 paper’s abstract was also located and describes a millennium reconstruction and the 1990s/1998 conclusion. The assessment’s attribution is consistent with the successor’s scope.

C. PUBLIC CAUSAL INTERPRETATION REPORTED BY NRC

Provenance: RETRIEVED.

Restatement:
The NRC says “many people interpreted this result as definitive evidence of anthropogenic causes of recent climate change.”

Layer:
Public reception / PR-media-type interpretation, as described by an assessment.

Attribution check:
The NRC does not present this as MBH98’s own conclusion. It identifies it as an interpretation made by others and later states that surface-temperature reconstructions are not the primary evidence for anthropogenic warming.

Classification:
Not a verified citing-text misattribution. No specific media or institutional sentence citing MBH98 for definitive anthropogenic causation was retrieved in this run, so this item cannot anchor PR/MEDIA as an established failure location.

Searches run
- MBH98 title and citation combined with “past millennium,” “warmest decade,” and “warmest year.”
- IPCC TAR Chapter 2 references to Mann, Bradley, and Hughes 1998/1999.
- National Research Council “Surface Temperature Reconstructions for the Last 2,000 Years” references to the 1998 and 1999 papers.
- Searches for assessment-language distinctions between the 600-year MBH98 reconstruction and the millennium MBH99 extension.

Scope note
The downstream search was targeted, not an exhaustive citation-context census. NONE FOUND is therefore not used for all PR, media, policy, or model layers.


5. WHERE THE INFLATION BEGINS
-----------------------------

Anchoring items and provenance states
1. MBH98 full-text quantified claim — RETRIEVED; FULL-TEXT-CONFIRMED.
2. NRC 2006 joint attribution and scope-disambiguating passages — RETRIEVED; CROSS-SOURCE-CONFIRMED.
3. IPCC TAR passage assigning the millennium annual/decadal claim to Mann et al. 1999 — RETRIEVED.

Within-source component
Transition:
- Measurement/model output: a Northern Hemisphere annual-mean reconstruction whose earliest interval uses 22 indicators, retains one eigenvector, and is reported to resolve about 40–50% of NH variance.
- Claim: named recent annual anomalies exceed every reconstructed annual anomaly back to 1400 at three standard errors, “roughly a 99.7% level of certainty.”

Failure location:
PAPER.

Meaning of classification:
The numerical confidence/ranking assertion is made by MBH98 itself. The classification only identifies where the inferential commitment enters the chain. It is not a finding that the inference is wrong.

Downstream component
- The retrieved millennium formulations are either attributed directly to MBH99 or jointly to MBH98/MBH99 in a document that distinguishes the papers’ scopes.
- Accordingly, a citation-layer misattribution of the millennium claim to MBH98 alone is not established by the checked sources.
- A broader causal interpretation circulated, according to NRC, but the specific citing sentence necessary to assign PR/MEDIA or CITATION as a failure location was not retrieved.

Downstream failure location:
Neither apparent in the selected, checked assessment attributions.

Overall classification for Field 6:
PAPER, because Field 6 targets MBH98’s own quantified claim rather than a downstream restatement.


6. WHICH CLAIM IS THIS AUDIT EVALUATING?
----------------------------------------

This audit evaluates MBH98’s claim that its reconstructed Northern Hemisphere annual-mean temperature anomalies for 1990, 1995, and 1997 were greater than those of every other year back to AD 1400 at three standard errors, or roughly 99.7% certainty.

Selection rationale
- One claim only.
- It is the source’s own claim.
- It is the most precisely quantified form available.
- It preserves the paper’s unit, geography, time boundary, and confidence language.
- It gives the construct-validity, internal-validity, and citation-network prosecutors the same bounded target.
- It does not bundle the separate forcing-attribution claim.
- It does not substitute the successor paper’s millennium claim.


